{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path (roughly 3–8 €/t), with larger cuts when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model is slow to recognize regime breaks (it keeps prices elevated after the market rolls over). If the last 5–10 days show a clear decline, force a steeper near-term slope down (front-load the correction in days 1–10).",
    "Insufficient drawdown magnitude: realized selloffs are deeper than forecast. Increase downside elasticity: when 10–20d realized volatility is elevated or recent peak-to-trough drop > ~5 €/t, amplify negative moves (e.g., scale forecasted daily changes downward by ~1.2–1.5x in the direction of the trend).",
    "Over-optimistic medium horizon: errors grow with horizon; days 15–30 are most overstated. Apply a horizon-dependent haircut that increases with lead time (small adjustment days 1–5, larger adjustment days 15–30).",
    "Mean reversion mis-specified: model reverts upward toward prior highs, but the market mean-reverts lower after a breakdown. Replace upward reversion with reversion toward a lower anchor (e.g., recent 10–20d average or a rolling support level) when the last-30d trend is negative.",
    "Post-spike overshoot: after high-price history windows (upper 70s/80s), the model continues to project rebounds; realized path continues falling. Cap rebounds: limit forecasted recoveries to a fraction of the prior drawdown (e.g., max 30–50% retrace) unless recent returns turn positive."
  ]
}
